TURNPIKE CHEMICALS LLC — LAKE CHARLES, LA

API 579 ASSESSMENT PROCEDURE — SITE GUIDANCE NOTE

  -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Document / Rev                      TC-MECH-PROC-0007 Rev 2
  ----------------------------------- -----------------------------------------------
  Issue date                          06/03/2026

  Originator                          James Merchant, Mechanical Integrity Engineer

  Checker                             Steve David, NDT Inspector (API 653/510)

  Approver                            Steven Lee, Engineering Manager

  Supersedes                          TC-MECH-PROC-0007 Rev 1

  Register maintainer                 Leona Cartwright
  -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------

1. Purpose

This procedure sets out how the Lake Charles site applies API 579-1 / ASME FFS-1 Fitness-For-Service methodology to aboveground storage tanks assessed for continued service under API 653. It states when an assessment is required, who may perform and certify one, and how the three assessment levels are selected.

It is written for site engineers preparing assessments and for the API 653 inspector who signs them. It does not replace API 579-1 itself; it records the site conventions that the code leaves to the operator, and the documentation the site requires on every assessment file.

An assessment is required when any of the following conditions is met:

-   A UT thickness survey reports a minimum measured thickness at or below the tabulated Level I acceptance thickness for the course or component;

-   A visible damage mechanism is identified during inspection (general or local metal loss, pitting, blister, crack-like flaw, weld misalignment, distortion, fire damage);

-   A change of service or process is identified on the asset;

-   An inspection interval extension is sought beyond the calculated remaining-life basis on file;

-   The API 653 inspector calls for one in an external or internal inspection report.

2. API 579 assessment levels

API 579-1 organises fitness-for-service into three assessment levels of increasing rigor and decreasing conservatism. Each level uses the same acceptance framework but differs in the quality of input data, the analytical technique, and the assumptions carried through the calculation.

2.1 Level I — screening

A Level I assessment is a conservative screening check using generic criteria, nominal geometry and the minimum measured thickness from the governing UT grid. It is designed to be run quickly on tabulated inputs, using formulae and lookup charts published in API 579-1 for each damage mechanism. Level I assumes worst-case placement of the flaw, worst-case loading combination, and nominal design values where measured values are absent.

Level I is conservative by design. The tabulated acceptance criteria are set to bound a wide population of tanks with a single generic answer, and the underlying assumptions systematically penalize the tank against a bespoke assessment.

Data required for Level I:

-   Minimum measured thickness at the governing grid point;

-   Nominal geometry: diameter, course height, nominal thickness by course;

-   Design pressure / hydrostatic head, design temperature, service fluid;

-   Governing damage mechanism as identified in the inspection report;

-   Applicable API 579-1 Part number for the mechanism (Part 4 general metal loss, Part 5 localized metal loss, Part 6 pitting, etc.).

2.2 Level II — actual geometry and measured data

A Level II assessment uses the actual as-built geometry of the component and the full measured thickness profile in place of the nominal values and minimum-thickness assumptions of Level I. It replaces the generic Level I acceptance table with the closed-form or tabulated evaluation procedures in API 579-1 that carry the actual flaw dimensions, actual thickness distribution and actual loading combination through the calculation.

Level II is the standard site-engineer assessment. It requires a competent engineer familiar with the API 579-1 procedure for the governing damage mechanism, and access to the full inspection dataset. It removes the bounding assumptions of Level I while remaining within closed-form analysis; no finite-element work is required.

Data required for Level II:

-   Full UT thickness profile across the grid, not only the minimum reading;

-   As-built geometry drawn from the tank construction record pack — actual course thicknesses, actual course heights, actual weld details;

-   per the site design wind data study

-   Corrosion rate, with the interval over which it was calculated;

-   Governing damage mechanism and applicable API 579-1 procedure.

2.3 Level III — detailed FEA-based

A Level III assessment uses detailed numerical analysis — typically finite-element analysis — to evaluate the component against the acceptance criteria of API 579-1. It is used where the geometry, loading or damage state is outside the range of the closed-form Level II procedures, where the tank is close to the Level II acceptance boundary and a more precise result would materially change the outcome, or where multiple interacting damage mechanisms cannot be handled by superposition.

Level III requires an engineer competent in non-linear structural FEA and in the API 579-1 analytical framework. It is commissioned rather than run in-line, and the analyst's scope, mesh, material model, load cases and acceptance basis are agreed in writing before work begins. Level III output is a full analysis report referenced from the FFS assessment file.

Data required for Level III:

-   Everything required for Level II, plus

-   A calibrated FE model of the component with the flaw or damage represented explicitly;

-   Material stress-strain data appropriate to the analysis (elastic, elastic-plastic, or creep) rather than tabulated allowables;

-   Documented mesh convergence and load-step convergence;

-   A written analysis basis agreed by the Engineering Manager before the model is built.

3. Level I conservatism

4. Data requirements

Every input to an FFS assessment shall be traceable to a named source document and its issue date. The basis for every input used shall be stated in the assessment.

Design inputs shall be traceable to the design data register.

The following inputs are always recorded on the face of the assessment:

-   Governing UT thickness survey — document number, revision, campaign date;

-   Governing UT thickness survey contractor and campaign reference;

-   Corrosion rate applied, with the basis (long-term average, short-term post-event, other) and the service interval over which it was calculated;

-   Minimum required thickness for the component under evaluation, with the calculation basis cited;

-   Design loading basis, with reference to the current site design wind data study and any superseded predecessor;

-   Applicable construction code edition for the tank;

-   Applicable in-service code;

-   Governing damage mechanism and the API 579-1 Part invoked.

5. Roles and competency

FFS assessments are prepared, reviewed and certified by three distinct roles.

Preparer. A mechanical engineer competent in the API 579-1 procedure for the governing damage mechanism. Junior engineers may draft assessments under supervision; the draft is not the signed record.

Reviewer / Checker. A second engineer independent of the preparer, or the Mechanical Integrity Engineer, who checks inputs, methodology, arithmetic and traceability. The reviewer signs the check block, not the certification block.

Certifier. The API 653 Aboveground Storage Tank Inspector or, for pressure vessels, the API 510 Inspector.

6. Documentation and records

An FFS assessment record on file at Turnpike Lake Charles shall include, at a minimum:

-   The controlled document header (document number, revision, issue date, originator, checker, approver);

-   Tank identifier in full (e.g. T-01-003), bund, service, applicable construction code edition;

-   The assessment level performed, named explicitly on the face of the record — Level I, Level II, or Level III;

-   All inputs listed in Section 4, each traceable to a source document with issue date;

-   The calculation itself, either in the body of the record or as an attached workbook referenced by document number and revision;

-   The acceptance criterion applied and the numerical result against it;

-   A conclusion stating whether the tank meets the criteria at the level performed;

-   The signature block of the API 653 or API 510 certifier;

-   The reviewer's check signature;

-   A cross-reference to the inspection report or event that triggered the assessment.

Records are filed under /Engineering/Integrity/Tanks/<tank identifier>/ by tank identifier and assessment date. Superseded revisions are not deleted; they are marked SUPERSEDED on the face and moved to the archive sub-folder, with the new revision cross-referring to the superseded one.

The register of FFS assessments is maintained by Leona Cartwright alongside the inspection certification register. An assessment that is not on the register is not on file.

7. Signatures

  --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Role              Name                                                           Signature         Date
  ----------------- -------------------------------------------------------------- ----------------- -----------------
  Originator        James Merchant, Mechanical Integrity Engineer                  [signed]          06/03/2026

  Checker           Steve David, API 653 Cert. No. 653-38472 (Expiry 02/20/2029)   [signed]          06/03/2026

  Approver          Steven Lee, Engineering Manager                                [signed]          06/03/2026
  --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

End of TC-MECH-PROC-0007 Rev 2. This document supersedes Rev 1 in its entirety. Comments and proposed revisions to James Merchant.

